# Copy reviewed dhcp_pull.py and dsm_apply.py side by side onto the NAS.
# The DSM helper requires root. Use a DSM user-defined scheduled task as root:
# /bin/python /volume1/dnsgrid/bin/dhcp_pull.py --config /volume1/dnsgrid/etc/dhcp_pull.ini
# For a supervised manual run, prefix that command with sudo.
# Verify the interpreter is Python 3.8+ and use your actual absolute paths.
# Non-root runs stop locally with requires_root (exit 1) before config/network/state.
# --help and pure schema helpers do not require root.
# No daemon, third-party packages, remote commands or configurable DSM executable.
#
# Scripts, import directory/parents, interpreter and executable dependencies must
# be trusted, root-owned and not writable by untrusted users, including via ACLs.
# Use a controlled working directory/environment. Audit stale __pycache__/bytecode;
# chown does not make untrusted code safe, and -B does not prevent cache loading.
# Prefer a new empty trusted code directory; do not blindly delete old caches.
# The root preflight does not check code integrity; imports happen before it.
# Config: single-link regular file, owner = effective UID (root), use mode 0600.
# Config/CA also accept 0400. State directories: same owner, mode 0700;
# state/lock/backup files: single-link regular files, root-owned, mode 0600.
# Ancestors must not be symlinks or group/world writable and must be owned by
# root or the effective UID (both UID 0 for supported runs); not necessarily 0700.
# This covers every ancestor from /, not just the immediate parent.
# Do not place files under /tmp or a shared directory.
# See README "NAS installation and schedule" for bounded installation/migration:
# inspect explicit paths and deployment-specific parents before chmod/chown.
# Preserve existing state.json, lock and backups when migrating from non-root;
# transfer verified private paths to root with 0700 directories/0600 files.
# Cover optional CA too. No recursive chmod/chown, blanket removal, changes to
# DSM helper/shared system parents or sudoers rules. Do not expose tokens.
# The state directory is created if absent; its parent must already exist.
# HTTPS is the default and verifies TLS certificates. HTTP is accepted only with
# allow_http_over_vpn = true, when the entire connection is restricted to the VPN.
# That setting is an operator assertion; the client does not verify VPN routing.
# A private CA file is optional for HTTPS.
# If supplied, ca_file must be a PEM file under the same private-file/ancestor
# rules as the config; it is bounded to 1 MiB and loaded from validated bytes.
# timeout bounds each socket operation. The body deadline is checked between
# reads, so a final blocking read can add up to one socket timeout; OS DNS
# resolution time is platform-controlled. DSM subprocesses have a 120s deadline.
# Authentication is a bearer-token header only; redirects and proxies are disabled.
#
# Exit codes: 0 verified/no-op + report delivered; 1 failure/report retry;
# 2 rejected/operator review; 3 another instance holds the lock.
# Logs are fixed English codes, never server content, credentials or exceptions.
#
# All runs read live DSM, including HTTP 304. Changed live reservations block
# writes. After reviewing DSM and backups, use --reconcile for ONE run to adopt
# or replace drift with the exact current publication. This does not bypass the
# empty/<50%-of-live-or-last-applied guard: that publication must explicitly
# carry destructive_authorized=true, including an intentional empty list.
# Never remove state blindly to bypass drift or destructive checks.
#
# Durable state separates fetched, applied, inflight, baseline and pending reports.
# Successful DSM verification is persisted before an applied report is sent.
# A failed report retries on the next successful fetch/live-read, without
# applying twice. Failed/rejected publications never advance the applied ETag.
# The last delivered status is also durable. After a failure or rejection,
# verified live equality emits a fresh applied report without another DSM write;
# an already-delivered applied report is not repeated on ordinary no-op polls.
# Reports count as delivered only after HTTP 200 with exact JSON {"status": ...}
# matching the pending status. HTML/malformed/mismatched receipts retain pending.
# New reports include report_id, a canonical lowercase hyphenated UUID.
# The UUID is durable with the pending report and unchanged on retries. New
# attempts/status transitions get new UUIDs, including on the same publication.
# Backend duplicate UUIDs never advance status pointers; a new verified success
# can clear an earlier failure on that same version. Legacy reports without
# report_id remain readable/retryable but cannot distinguish recurring attempts.
# Backend retains the newest 50 receipts plus last-attempt, last-failure and
# latest-applied pins (at most 53/site). Fresh UUIDs remain accepted for retained
# publications; there is no permanent per-publication attempt cap.
# UUID deduplication covers only retained receipts. An expired unknown UUID
# is treated as a new event; do not replay manually archived acknowledgements.
# The current pending latest attempt is pinned by the backend when accepted.
# Undelivered pending reports are always retained/retried, even with --reconcile
# and even after a different latest publication becomes available.
# A write-ahead attempt permits crash recovery by checking live DSM equality;
# unchanged pre-write baseline permits a safe retry; any other mismatch requires
# operator reconciliation. A crash between the external
# DSM write and local verification is inherently nontransactional; equality
# proves current state, not who wrote it. Concurrent external changes remain
# possible between the final baseline read and DSM set; avoid other writers.
# Backups are private and durable before writes. Keep the newest 20 complete
# backups; pruning never removes the current run's backup, even after clock
# rollback. Unexpected files, symlinks or non-private backups stop writes.
# Keep state and backups on persistent local storage, not a network mount.
# A structurally valid, checksum-verified envelope for this site with the wrong
# configured interface/subnet is reported rejected without applying or storing
# its payload as fetched. Malformed/untrusted identity or checksums are not acked.

[dhcp_pull]
base_url = https://dnsgrid.example.invalid
allow_http_over_vpn = false
site_id = 1
ifname = eth0
subnet = 192.168.1.0/24
token = CHANGE_ME_BEFORE_USE
state_dir = /var/lib/dnsgrid-dhcp
timeout = 30
# ca_file = /private/path/internal-ca.pem
